WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.774
In this video, we explore how we investigate
the root cause of a test problem with the

00:00:04.774 --> 00:00:05.940
MachinePulseAI Agent.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.229
We follow the complete process in the
application, from selecting a model and

00:00:11.229 --> 00:00:15.520
sending a request to running analyses and
preparing the final report.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.860
Let's explore the application's capabilities
through an automotive test.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.960
In this example, an engineer feels vibration
in the steering wheel while braking.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.680
We use the data collected during the test to
investigate its source.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:35.060
First, we select the Qwen3 Coder 30B model
running on our computer and connect to it.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.880
In this example, the Agent uses a local model
to conduct the analysis.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.800
We also add the documents used in the
investigation to the Knowledge Base.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.935
These include sensor locations, the mounting
log and the test limit for disc thickness

00:00:47.935 --> 00:00:48.500
variation.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:54.505
The documents are stored locally and can be
reused for relevant questions in later

00:00:54.505 --> 00:00:55.120
sessions.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.790
We describe the vibration felt during braking
and give the Agent the driving recordings,

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:02.980
repeat test and disc measurements.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.360
We ask it to compare possible causes and
report its findings.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.520
Then we send the request.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.528
The Agent opens the recordings and uses RAG to
consult the technical documents in the

00:01:15.528 --> 00:01:16.320
Knowledge Base.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.292
It checks data quality, builds the analysis
workflow and cleans the documented invalid

00:01:21.292 --> 00:01:23.780
measurement code with the engineer's approval.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:29.160
We follow its work through the assistant's
messages and the analysis windows.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.220
First, we examine the difference between
braking and coast at the same wheel speed.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.842
Using Order Tracking, the Agent compares these
two sections and shows a pronounced increase

00:01:40.842 --> 00:01:44.160
in steering first-order vibration during
braking.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.420
It calculates both values using the same
peak-amplitude convention.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.920
This controls for speed and focuses the
investigation on braking-related excitation.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.256
In the repeat test, vibration persists in
independent measurements despite the sensor

00:01:57.256 --> 00:01:58.260
being remounted.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.820
This weakens an explanation based solely on
one sensor's mounting.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.959
The Agent also finds that the front-left disc
thickness variation reaches twice the

00:02:07.959 --> 00:02:09.220
documented test limit.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:13.500
Considering both findings, it turns toward a
braking-related explanation.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.825
The engineer checks the Agent's initial
interpretations against the measurements and

00:02:17.825 --> 00:02:19.320
makes the necessary corrections.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:24.267
In the final assessment, front-left disc
thickness variation stands out as a likely

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:25.340
excitation source.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:30.560
The Agent also considers steering resonance as
a possible amplifier of the vibration.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.286
Confirming this explanation requires a
physical inspection and another test after

00:02:35.286 --> 00:02:36.000
intervention.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.940
When the investigation is complete, we open
the final report.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.908
The report brings together the analyses,
comparison plots and reference documents used

00:02:44.908 --> 00:02:45.720
by the Agent.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.300
The engineer can review the evidence and plan
the next physical inspection.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:53.020
This example uses synthetic data.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.120
To see the same process with your own test
recording, request a demo on our website.
